    },
    {
      "id": "4n_mgmg1fYdwMoh5q5pRd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 418.45059903705834,
      "y": 5332.420476331538,
      "width": 407,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpS",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2026885998,
      "version": 39,
      "versionNonce": 1409573678,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769929945652,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now let's start with django framework",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now let's start with django framework",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "WyjnZwpXbWz5YaYaiiQMX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 419.6361534250439,
      "y": 5408.298268246525,
      "width": 792,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 542026290,
      "version": 173,
      "versionNonce": 747544114,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930350043,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now before starting with django we need to start creating a virtual env \nand let's see why it's necessary to create a virtual env",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now before starting with django we need to start creating a virtual env \nand let's see why it's necessary to create a virtual env",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "WItBptXkWEikbZyVaZT22",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 427.98922058072844,
      "y": 5481.38315367528,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpU",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 262027698,
      "version": 56,
      "versionNonce": 1214334510,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930370469,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When developing software with Python, a basic approach is to install\nPython on your machine, install all your required libraries via the\nterminal, write all your code in a single .py file or notebook, and\nrun your Python program in the terminal.\n\nThis is a common approach for a lot of beginners and many people\ntransitioning from working with Python for data analytics.\n\nThis works fine for simple Python scripting projects. But in complex\nsoftware development projects, like building a Python library, an\nAPI, or software development kit, often you will be working with\nmultiple files, multiple packages, and dependencies. As a result,\nyou will need to isolate your Python development environment for\nthat particular project",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When developing software with Python, a basic approach is to install Python on your machine, install all your required libraries via the terminal, write all your code in a single .py file or notebook, and run your Python program in the terminal.\n\nThis is a common approach for a lot of beginners and many people transitioning from working with Python for data analytics.\n\nThis works fine for simple Python scripting projects. But in complex software development projects, like building a Python library, an API, or software development kit, often you will be working with multiple files, multiple packages, and dependencies. As a result, you will need to isolate your Python development environment for that particular project",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hobtUWN60cqT6Y4a1vpqZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 426.21082568138263,
      "y": 5867.7887659123635,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1331050290,
      "version": 80,
      "versionNonce": 406947502,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930404769,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Consider this scenario: you are working on app A, using your system\ninstalled Python and you pip install packageX version 1.0 to your\nglobal Python library. Then you switch to project B on your local\nmachine, and you install the same packageX but version 2.0, which\nhas some breaking changes between version 1.0 and 2.0.\n\nWhen you go back to run your app A, you get all sorts of errors, and\nyour app does not run. This is a scenario you can run into when\nbuilding software with Python. And to get around this, we can use\nvirtual environments.\n\nThis tutorial will cover everything you need to know about virtual\nenvironments and how to set one up with Virtualenv.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Consider this scenario: you are working on app A, using your system installed Python and you pip install packageX version 1.0 to your global Python library. Then you switch to project B on your local machine, and you install the same packageX but version 2.0, which has some breaking changes between version 1.0 and 2.0.\n\nWhen you go back to run your app A, you get all sorts of errors, and your app does not run. This is a scenario you can run into when building software with Python. And to get around this, we can use virtual environments.\n\nThis tutorial will cover everything you need to know about virtual environments and how to set one up with Virtualenv.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Gjt94Og9ZFY_Twec9UBR6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 423.24674975931646,
      "y": 6224.611633795956,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpW",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 79928686,
      "version": 57,
      "versionNonce": 1871198834,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930421669,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is a Virtual Environment?\nPython's official documentation says:\n\n\"A virtual environment is a Python environment such that the Python\ninterpreter, libraries and scripts installed into it are isolated\nfrom those installed in other virtual environments, and (by default)\nany libraries installed in a “system” Python, i.e., one which is\ninstalled as part of your operating system\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is a Virtual Environment?\nPython's official documentation says:\n\n\"A virtual environment is a Python environment such that the Python interpreter, libraries and scripts installed into it are isolated from those installed in other virtual environments, and (by default) any libraries installed in a “system” Python, i.e., one which is installed as part of your operating system\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "MGggmhhPSJujbnDp50l_z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 419.6901499127272,
      "y": 6458.019074367106,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 59722286,
      "version": 65,
      "versionNonce": 1698277298,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930436069,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "To break this down, when you activate a virtual environment for your\nproject, your project becomes its own self contained application,\nindependent of the system installed Python and its modules.\n\nYour new virtual environment has its own pip to install libraries,\nits own libraries folder, where new libraries are added, and its own\nPython interpreter for the Python version you used to activate the\nenvironment.\n\nWith this new environment, your application becomes self-contained\nand you get some benefits such as:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "To break this down, when you activate a virtual environment for your project, your project becomes its own self contained application, independent of the system installed Python and its modules.\n\nYour new virtual environment has its own pip to install libraries, its own libraries folder, where new libraries are added, and its own Python interpreter for the Python version you used to activate the environment.\n\nWith this new environment, your application becomes self-contained and you get some benefits such as:",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "NKcbpMh8tR1vsxCzh8H8K",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 425.02539792813184,
      "y": 6744.088309543881,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1331929326,
      "version": 106,
      "versionNonce": 1122862066,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930496568,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-Your development environment is contained within your project,\nbecomes isolated, and does not interfere with your system installed\nPython or other virtual environments\n\n-You can create a new virtual environment for multiple Python\nversions\n\n-You are able to download packages into your project without admin\nprivileges\n\n-You can easily package your application and share with other\ndevelopers to replicate\n\n-You can easily create a list of dependencies and sub dependencies\nin a file, for your project, which makes it easy for other\ndevelopers to replicate and install all the dependencies used within\nyour environment",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-Your development environment is contained within your project, becomes isolated, and does not interfere with your system installed Python or other virtual environments\n\n-You can create a new virtual environment for multiple Python versions\n\n-You are able to download packages into your project without admin privileges\n\n-You can easily package your application and share with other developers to replicate\n\n-You can easily create a list of dependencies and sub dependencies in a file, for your project, which makes it easy for other developers to replicate and install all the dependencies used within your environment",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "WaN-nFogoRmF6WmzIb6zY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 421.79804878126356,
      "y": 7200.643276783048,
      "width": 726,
      "height": 275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 885766318,
      "version": 60,
      "versionNonce": 653191282,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930509848,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Using virtual environments is recommended for software development\nprojects that generally grow out of a single Python script, and\nPython provides multiple ways of creating and using a virtual\nenvironment.\n\nIn the sections below, we will walk through how to set up your\nvirtual environment, using venv, which gives you a lot more low\nlevel control of your environment.\n\nAnother common way to set up your virtual environment is to use\npipenv, which is a more high level approach.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Using virtual environments is recommended for software development projects that generally grow out of a single Python script, and Python provides multiple ways of creating and using a virtual environment.\n\nIn the sections below, we will walk through how to set up your virtual environment, using venv, which gives you a lot more low level control of your environment.\n\nAnother common way to set up your virtual environment is to use pipenv, which is a more high level approach.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hsix3IVCaZ5WLMhoalnDx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 423.90613760190206,
      "y": 7517.207407296437,
      "width": 704,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpa",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 269351982,
      "version": 69,
      "versionNonce": 712033202,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930542033,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to Install a Virtual Environment using Venv\nVirtualenv is a tool to set up your Python environments. Since\nPython 3.3, a subset of it has been integrated into the standard\nlibrary under the venv module. You can install venv to your host\nPython by running this command in your terminal:\n\n1.pip install virtualenv",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to Install a Virtual Environment using Venv\nVirtualenv is a tool to set up your Python environments. Since Python 3.3, a subset of it has been integrated into the standard library under the venv module. You can install venv to your host Python by running this command in your terminal:\n\n1.pip install virtualenv",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "BoYgfbFhrlFKC8XKmkSyx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 427.5611003952798,
      "y": 7745.536174108626,
      "width": 737,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 529680626,
      "version": 61,
      "versionNonce": 2087838130,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930574342,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "To use venv in your project, in your terminal, create a new project\nfolder, cd to the project folder in your terminal, and run the\nfollowing command:\n\n\n2.python<version> -m venv <virtual-environment-name>",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "To use venv in your project, in your terminal, create a new project folder, cd to the project folder in your terminal, and run the following command:\n\n\n2.python<version> -m venv <virtual-environment-name>",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hxDmXk32qLbF5iANkwJYq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 425.02514465866216,
      "y": 7932.038681622262,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpc",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1982822702,
      "version": 58,
      "versionNonce": 1162173038,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930608504,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Like so:\n\n mkdir projectA\n cd projectA\n python3.8 -m venv env\n\n\nWhen you check the new projectA folder, you will notice that a new\nfolder called env has been created. env is the name of our virtual\nenvironment, but it can be named anything you want.\n\nIf we check the contents of env for a bit, on a Mac you will see a\nbin folder. You will also see scripts that are typically used to\ncontrol your virtual environment, such as activate and pip to\ninstall libraries, and the Python interpreter for the Python version\nyou installed, and so on. (This folder will be called Scripts on\nwindows).\n\nThe lib folder will contain a list of libraries that you have\ninstalled. If you take a look at it, you will see a list of the\nlibraries that come by default with the virtual environment.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Like so:\n\n mkdir projectA\n cd projectA\n python3.8 -m venv env\n\n\nWhen you check the new projectA folder, you will notice that a new folder called env has been created. env is the name of our virtual environment, but it can be named anything you want.\n\nIf we check the contents of env for a bit, on a Mac you will see a bin folder. You will also see scripts that are typically used to control your virtual environment, such as activate and pip to install libraries, and the Python interpreter for the Python version you installed, and so on. (This folder will be called Scripts on windows).\n\nThe lib folder will contain a list of libraries that you have installed. If you take a look at it, you will see a list of the libraries that come by default with the virtual environment.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "VDekQvpHg1lxeBWHnlyz8",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 422.6001660297427,
      "y": 7973.916319089474,
      "width": 343.22844268744495,
      "height": 103.14633430574759,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpd",
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 1408240946,
      "version": 55,
      "versionNonce": 701098926,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930612751,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "DBk0WQNQIf1r1eZjAOmGy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 422.6540358826911,
      "y": 8553.504809418082,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bpe",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1849297330,
      "version": 103,
      "versionNonce": 314879086,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769930629531,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to Activate the Virtual Environment\nNow that you have created the virtual environment, you will need to\nactivate it before you can use it in your project. On a mac, to\nactivate your virtual environment, run the code below:\n\nsource env/bin/activate\n\nThis will activate your virtual environment. Immediately, you will\nnotice that your terminal path includes env, signifying an activated\nvirtual environment.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to Activate the Virtual Environment\nNow that you have created the virtual environment, you will need to activate it before you can use it in your project. On a mac, to activate your virtual environment, run the code below:\n\nsource env/bin/activate\n\nThis will activate your virtual environment. Immediately, you will notice that your terminal path includes env, signifying an activated virtual environment.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o56cuJlmF_JM3nfWFV-h6",
