        1,
        1
      ],
      "crop": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wfWvs_Tp98iKDTrCN-Jjn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 392.42138591118044,
      "y": 13915.520621154637,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bq3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 800422515,
      "version": 46,
      "versionNonce": 1691095005,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1769931850565,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1.Browser Sends Request: The browser sends an HTTP request to the\nweb server (e.g., typing http://127.0.0.1:8000/).\n\n2.Web Server Receives It: The server (like Nginx or Apache) serves\nstatic files directly or forwards other requests to a WSGI/ASGI\nserver such as Gunicorn or Daphne.\nRequest Reaches Django: The Web Server Gateway Interface\n(WSGI)/Asynchronous Server Gateway Interface (ASGI) server converts\nthe request into a Python-friendly format and passes it through \nDjango’s middleware before it reaches your app.\n\n3.URL Routing: Django checks urls.py to find the correct view\nfunction for the request, turning it into an HttpRequest object.\nView Handles Logic: The view processes the request - querying the\ndatabase, rendering templates, or returning data (like JSON).\n\n4.Response Created: The view returns an HttpResponse object, which\nDjango converts into an HTTP response.\n\n5.Response Sent to Browser: The response passes back through the\nserver and is displayed by the browser as a web page.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1.Browser Sends Request: The browser sends an HTTP request to the web server (e.g., typing http://127.0.0.1:8000/).\n\n2.Web Server Receives It: The server (like Nginx or Apache) serves static files directly or forwards other requests to a WSGI/ASGI server such as Gunicorn or Daphne.\nRequest Reaches Django: The Web Server Gateway Interface (WSGI)/Asynchronous Server Gateway Interface (ASGI) server converts the request into a Python-friendly format and passes it through \nDjango’s middleware before it reaches your app.\n\n3.URL Routing: Django checks urls.py to find the correct view function for the request, turning it into an HttpRequest object.\nView Handles Logic: The view processes the request - querying the database, rendering templates, or returning data (like JSON).\n\n4.Response Created: The view returns an HttpResponse object, which Django converts into an HTTP response.\n\n5.Response Sent to Browser: The response passes back through the server and is displayed by the browser as a web page.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
