      "updated": 1769937006420,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1.Next, we’ll navigate to views.py. This file will contain a number of different views, and we\ncan think of a view for now as one page the user might like to see. To create our first view,\nwe’ll write a function that takes in a request. For now, we’ll simply return an HttpResponse (A\nvery simple response that includes a response code of 200 and a string of text that can be\ndisplayed in a web browser) of “Hello, World”. In order to do this, we have include from\ndjango.http import HttpResponse. Our file now looks like:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1.Next, we’ll navigate to views.py. This file will contain a number of different views, and we can think of a view for now as one page the user might like to see. To create our first view, we’ll write a function that takes in a request. For now, we’ll simply return an HttpResponse (A very simple response that includes a response code of 200 and a string of text that can be displayed in a web browser) of “Hello, World”. In order to do this, we have include from django.http import HttpResponse. Our file now looks like:",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
